In the above code, for each method under test (`PUT` stands for Pex Unit Test), we have put a Debug.Assert() statement per each output that we wish to validate. The expression inside the Debug.Assert() ensures the expected result based on the method logic. The assumptions made are taken from the description of the methods and the expected results.--------------------To solve this problem, you can use the `Regex` class in the `System.Text.RegularExpressions` namespace, which allows you to search for patterns in strings. Here is a simple snippet on how you can do it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string code = File.ReadAllText("test.txt"); // put your file name here
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(code, @"^([\s]*)\/\/\/ @@@(.*?)\n");

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.Write($"{match.Value}\n");
        }
    }
}
```

In the above example, "test.txt" is the path of the file containing the code. It will print all the comment lines in the code that start with "@@@". 

You could change the regular expression to include more complex conditions if you think they are appropriate.

Remember that the actual file name must be place in the "test.txt". You should replace it with your own file. You should place your file into the same path as the executable project.--------------------This is an example of how you can extract assert statements from the given C# code:

